text tphap2 "\ncurl -fsSL "
text tphap3 " | git am</pre>\n<p><a href=\""
text tphap4 "\">Raw patch (mbox)</a> &middot; <a href=\""
text tphap5 "\">Diff</a></p></div>\n"
text tphsg1 "<div class=\"box\"><h3>Signature</h3>\n<p class=\"meta\">The author's key <code>"
text tphsg2 "</code> signed this statement with <code>ssh-keygen -Y sign -n interplane-patch</code>; the forge checked the signature before it kept the patch. The lines are: format, repository, base, SHA-256 of the mbox, SHA-256 of the title, a line feed and the description.</p>\n<pre class=\"hit\">"
text tphsg3 "<pre class=\"hit\">"
text tphdv "</div>\n"
text tphdf1 "<h3>Changes</h3>\n<pre class=\"hit\">"
text tphdfc "<p class=\"meta\">The diff is longer than this page shows; the raw diff has all of it.</p>\n"
text tphn1 "<div class=\"box\"><h2>Open a patch</h2>\n<p>A patch is a set of commits you propose for this project. You send it from your own clone with the <code>interplane</code> program; your key signs it on your computer and never leaves it. The owner applies a patch by hand.</p>\n<pre class=\"so-cmd\">interplane patch "
text tphn2 "</pre>\n<p class=\"meta\">Run it inside a git work tree with your commits on top of this project's main branch. It shows what will be sent (base, commits, files, bytes, title) and sends only after you type yes. Limits: 100 commits, 2 MiB of <code>git format-patch</code> output, no binary patches.</p>\n</div>\n"
